  "query_preview": "..(Thursday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 332 PM EDT TUE SEP 22 2026\n\nThe long term period will bring pleasant fall weather to eastern \nKentucky. At the start of the period, an upper level trough will \nbe located to the east of the forecast area, with fairly amplified\nmidlevel ridging over the Great Plains. This ridge will keep us \nin a fairly stagnant yet delightful pattern for the duration of \nthe forecast period. At the surface, low end rain chances (around \n20%) will linger through Thursday evening, particularly near the \nTN line as the trough departs; otherwise, expect increasing \nsunshine throughout the day. With a strong coastal low in the \nAtlantic and building high pressure in the Great Lakes, winds will\nbecome increasingly northeasterly on Thursday, bringing in cooler\nand drier air. Dewpoints will plummet into the 50s and low 60s \nThursday, before dipping into the low 50s and then 40s by the \nweekend. High temperatures will also be very reminiscent of a true\nfall, ranging from the upper 60s to upper 70s across the forecast\narea from Thursday through the weekend. Temperatures will be \nwarmest in the southwest, closer to the ridge.\n\nGiven stubborn ridging over the central CONUS, weather will \nremain clear and dry throughout the period. Ridge valley splits \nwill be favored each night, providing an additional chill that \nshould further enhance that beautiful fall feeling. MOS guidance \nsuggests lows could dip into the mid 40s in sheltered valley \nlocations on Friday morning, and this seems reasonable given \nexpected clear skies and a weak pressure gradient. MinTs could be \neven cooler as dewpoints continue to drop going into the weekend, \nwith low 40s possible in the most sheltered locations. Even as \nmodel spread increases somewhat early next week, confidence \nremains high that our dry pattern should persist, with virtually \nall guidance keeping PoPs to near nonexistent levels. Temperatures\nmay increase somewhat early next week as ridging attempts to \nbuild eastward, with the warmest deterministic guidance suggesting\nhighs could perhaps reach the low 80s by the end of the period. \nEven so, no impactful weather is anticipated.",
